 if  people are coming to the border which  they will be um that those third  countries are able to deal fairly and  efficiently with those people and so  we're very much in discussions with them  of course part of this  is um ensuring that the taliban  do what the u.n security council  resolution requires them to do namely to  offer safe passage to people who are  rightly traveling to the borders to um  uh to seek sanctuary and what do we do  what how far are we prepared to go with  the taliban if they renege on promises  that they they have already made in  terms of safe passage  so simon gas uh has it's been announced  overnight simon gas the prime minister's  representative on afghan uh resettlement  is um very special envoy is very much uh  there in doha at the moment discussing  with senior taliban figures  on the future and i i don't want to in  any way uh impinge on those discussions  but the international community is  watching very closely what the taliban  is doing and will do over the next uh  weeks and months  and the foreign secretary and others  have been clear that we will use every  lever at our disposal diplomatic and  otherwise to ensure that the taliban  lives up to the resolution that was  passed by the security council  as as mentioned before of course those  relocating here under arab they have the  afghan relocation program i mean they  they are people who have assisted us you  know in our fight in afghanistan and and  their families and and people will not  begrudge them  being helped when they reach this  country but but do you not accept  that you have in essence now created  another tier another class perhaps  within our immigration and asylum system  well i don't think anyone watching the  scenes that we've seen over the last  couple of weeks could have failed to  have been moved by the  terror the desperation the anguish the  fear that people  were experiencing  not just outside the gates of the  airport but also of course across the  country and so i very much and i have  complete confidence that as you say uh  british people will understand that and  will want to respond and insurance but  do you do you accept that that they are  going to be treated differently by the  immigration and asylum program to anyone  else  well we have already the model for this  with the syrian resettlement scheme uh  as i say you know that was set up in  2015 um that we went to great efforts to  ensure that accommodation and other  services were already set up uh in  advance of syrians being lifted from  refugee camps to come to this country  for safe sanctuary um  these circumstances are obviously pretty  different not least because we've had to  evacuate people at such a fast pace into  the uk  but i would hope that given the um  the effort the work that  these translators these security guards  these  afghan army  members have done for us over the last  two decades the british soldiers they  will have worked with the lives  potentially some of them will have saved  um i'm very much hoping that we'll be  able to as i say do right by them just  as they did right by us just very  specifically on on the re-housing of  those who are coming here  do we do we really have the capacity to  re-home what 20 000 plus  afghans over the next five years when we  heard from local government association  chairman james jameson earlier you know  the nature of the refugees we're seeing  significantly larger families housing  stock as you well know is at a premium  in every part of the country  particularly when we get to the larger  sizes and we're we're kind of giving  five million top up payment to local  authorities that's not going to quite  cover it alone is it  look i want to be straight with people  this is a very very difficult challenge  and we are very very aware that of the  pressures on the housing market and  housing  lists already that is why  we have announced this as you say top-up  fund to help councils find larger  properties for  afghan families but it comes back to  integration again  this policy will succeed if we welcome  and we integrate people into our  societies and we get them settled into  accommodation help their children get  into schools get them registered at gps  and so on and then they can begin to  pay back into our society and i've heard  some wonderful stories over the last few  days you know people who have fought  with the british army have translated  for the british army determined uh when  they can to if they're allowed to join  the british army  others who very much want to  study so that they can continue their  medical careers and so on there have  been some really really inspirational um  stories that uh we've heard from people  who have  and so we really really want to achieve  that  i just want to squeeze in just a just a  just a kind of a final comment of course  afghans fleeing the country will be  trying to get to different parts of the  world for safety by a variety of means  we understand of course from this  government that in future you are  seeking  to deny asylum to people who arrive in  the uk having passed through other safe  countries it would seem that at some  point they're not too distant future  there will be hundreds if not thousands  of afghans who have made their way  through safe countries coming to the uk  and trying to claim asylum will we truly  deny them asylum at that point because  they have passed through safe countries  we are absolutely determined to crack  down on the illegal people trafficking  gangs that we see exploiting the channel  exploiting people um for profit it is  incredibly dangerous for people to make  these journeys i mean i my previous  career i used to prosecute criminals for  a living and and uh some of the cr the  migration cases i saw at the time were  truly truly horrific and so we want to  stop that um and we've got to send this  very clear message out to the people  traffickers  that if they do this if they're caught  then they will be dealt with in our  justice system  and when you just just to underline how  dangerous it is i mean very recently uh  someone sadly died in the channel trying  to make their journey across so this is  why  as part of this package we're saying  look we won't put up with illegal  immigration but please please use safe  and legal routes and that's why the  afghan resettlement scheme is so  important and taking you know what  little time we've taken to set it up  properly is vital because we want to  help people  who you know should come to this country  want to help them come here legally and  safely as indeed we did with the syrian  resettlement scheme
 

